## Reusable Findings

1. **Five-category agentic risk taxonomy as a coding frame for P1/P4 (feeds S4/S1):** The framework's top-level structure — (A) Individual AI Agent Risks (5 risks/37 controls), (B) Multiple AI Agent Risks (4/27), (C) AI Agent Security Threats (8/62), (D) AI Agent Governance Failures (8/81), (E) Human Factors for AI Agents (5/35) — is a ready-made **coding scheme** for classifying how Indian BFSI regulatory circulars (RBI FREE-AI, SEBI AI guidance) and the broader Policy Dump corpus distribute their attention across individual-agent, multi-agent, security, governance, and human-factors risk domains. A gap analysis (e.g., "RBI FREE-AI covers categories A/D/E but is largely silent on B — multi-agent orchestration risks") would be a directly citable P3/P4 contribution.

2. **Governance Failures category dominates control volume (81/242 = 33%) (feeds S1/Governance_Debt):** The framework allocates more controls to "AI Agent Governance Failures" (Risks 18–25: vendor/API instability, collateral damage, cost overruns, incident management, accountability/explainability, board oversight, regulatory risk, external disclosures) than to any other category — nearly double the "Security Threats" category (62) despite security typically dominating practitioner attention. This **resource-allocation signal** (governance > security in a 2025 industry framework) is a useful comparator when assessing whether Indian regulatory guidance is similarly governance-weighted or remains security/technical-control-heavy — a potential [[Institutional_Coherence]] gap if regulatory emphasis diverges from industry practitioner emphasis.

3. **"Multi-agent" risk category (B) as an explicit gap-marker for current Indian regulation (feeds S4/P3):** Risk 09 ("Uncontrolled Agent Replication") and Risk 08 ("Inter-Agent Orchestration and Communication") explicitly address risks that only arise once multiple autonomous agents interact (orchestrator subversion, agent identity confusion, collusion/wargaming — cf. Risk 06, 15.11). As of the corpus's current coverage, no RBI/SEBI/MeitY circular explicitly addresses multi-agent orchestration risk — this framework's Category B is a **forward-looking reference point** for identifying this as an emerging regulatory blind spot in India, directly supporting [[Agentic_AI_Governance]] as a live research theme (P3/P4).

4. **Board-level oversight (Risk 23) and California-specific regulatory tracking (Control 24.13) as comparative regulatory-design exemplars (feeds S1/Regulatory_Parallelism):** The framework's explicit "California AI Law Alignment" control (mapping SB-53 frontier-AI safety frameworks, SB-243 chatbot disclosure, CCPA/CPPA ADMT rules, and Civil Rights Department automated-decision-system rules to named controls) demonstrates a **jurisdiction-stacking design pattern** — a single control framework maintaining parallel "tracks" for different regulatory regimes. This is a reusable structural comparator for [[Regulatory_Parallelism]] when assessing whether India's emerging AI governance (DPDP Rules, RBI FREE-AI, sectoral guidance) could or should adopt a similar "jurisdictional track" architecture given India's own multi-regulator landscape (RBI/SEBI/IRDAI/MeitY).

5. **"Moral Legitimacy" and "Legal Protection of Fundamental Rights" (Risks 29–30) as the framework's most explicitly normative/rights-based controls (feeds S1/fsQCA):** Unlike the largely technical/operational controls in Categories A–D, Risk 29 (reject AI-as-moral-agent; name an accountable human for every AI-touched decision; avoid anthropomorphic branding) and Risk 30 (fundamental rights impact assessments, proportionality/"definitional balancing" tests, rights promotion/demotion tracking) introduce **explicitly rights-based, EU-fundamental-rights-style language** into an industry (not regulatory) framework. This convergence of industry practice with EU AI Act-style fundamental-rights assessment methodology is a useful **fsQCA condition candidate** — "presence/absence of fundamental-rights-impact-assessment requirement" as a configurational condition distinguishing more vs. less rights-protective AI governance regimes across jurisdictions.

